  "notes": [
    "mdpi / frontiers vary a lot per journal: kept at tier 2 but leaning heavily on citation adjustment.",
    "github.com sits at tier 4 and is moved mostly by actual star count.",
    "wikipedia is tier 5; SKILL.md instructs using it only to walk footnotes back to primary sources.",
    "consulting/market decks (mckinsey, bcg, bain, deloitte, pwc) moved to tier 4 and statista to tier 4: they are marketing-facing secondary aggregation, not primary research. gartner/forrester/idc kept one notch higher at tier 3 because they are the cited source of record in enterprise IT.",
    "mdpi moved to tier 3: per-journal quality varies too much to sit alongside springer/wiley.",
    "news.ycombinator.com and lesswrong moved to tier 5 (aggregator/community); actual signal comes from the hackernews engagement term, which community_opinion mode weights heavily.",
    "academia.edu unblocked to tier 5: it is author self-archiving, not a scraper mirror.",
    "vendor/language/framework documentation sits at tier 3 (primary but self-interested); standards bodies and CVE/vulnerability registries at tier 1."
  ]
